Eager: every map runs, then every select
    map(1) map(2) map(3) map(4) select(10) select(20) select(30) select(40)
 1. (1..4).map { }.select { }                                [20, 30, 40]
Lazy: one element through both blocks, and it stops at the second hit
    map(1) select(10) map(2) select(20) map(3) select(30)
 2. (1..4).lazy.map { }.select { }.first(2)                  [20, 30]

An endless source, which only a lazy chain can map over
 3. (1..Float::INFINITY).lazy.map { }.select { }.first(3)    [6, 12, 18]
 4. (1..).lazy, same chain; map calls it took                [[6, 12, 18], 9]
 5. (1..).lazy.take_while { |x| x < 4 }.to_a                 [1, 2, 3]
 6. (1..).lazy.each_slice(2).first(2)                        [[1, 2], [3, 4]]
 7. (1..).lazy.filter_map { x * 2 if x.even? }.first(3)      [4, 8, 12]
 8. (1..).lazy.with_index.map { |x, i| x * i }.first(3)      [0, 2, 6]
 9. (1..).lazy.zip("a".."c").first(2)                        [[1, "a"], [2, "b"]]
10. (1..).lazy.map { |x| x % 3 }.uniq.first(3)               [1, 2, 0]
11. (1..).lazy.flat_map { |x| [x, -x] }.first(4)             [1, -1, 2, -2]
12. (1..).lazy.drop_while { |x| x < 5 }.first(2)             [5, 6]

Which calls stay lazy, and which ones run the chain
13. lazy.class, lazy.map { }.class                           [Enumerator::Lazy, Enumerator::Lazy]
14. lazy.take(2).class, lazy.first(2).class                  [Enumerator::Lazy, Array]
15. (1..).lazy.map { |x| x * x }.take(3).force               [1, 4, 9]
16. lazy.eager.class, lazy.eager.map { }.class               [Enumerator, Array]
17. (1..).lazy.include?(5), (1..3).lazy.map { x * 2 }.sum    [true, 12]
18. (1..).lazy.size, [1,2,3].lazy.map {}.size, .select {}.size [Infinity, 3, nil]
19. e = (1..).lazy.map { x * 2 }; e.next, e.next             [2, 4]
20. Enumerator::Lazy.ancestors.take(3)                       [Enumerator::Lazy, Enumerator, Enumerable]
